**Verdict: research is ~90% sufficient to spec. A handful of concrete gaps would degrade or block parts of the build; none are fatal, but two are design-blocking decisions that need one more datum each.**

**Design-blocking contradictions**

- **"Spam" per taxonomy vs "noncompliant" per BIP-110 disagree wildly, and the whole battle metaphor depends on which one drives it.** Runes = ~56% of all txs but small runestones PASS BIP-110 (rule 1 allows ≤83B OP_RETURN); true BIP-110-noncompliant txs (ordinals/BRC-20 ~1.3%, stamps a few/block, >83B OP_RETURN ~23 outputs/block) are roughly 0.1–0.2 tx/s — one attacker every 5–10 seconds. Check: run the existing rawscan.py classifier over the same 7 blocks with rules 1–7 flat and count noncompliant tx/s, so the spec can decide (a) which classifier defines the horde (protocol vs BIP-110 vs both as unit types), and (b) whether the sim needs to pad live action rates.
- **The defender army has NO identified live data source.** Attackers stream from the WS, but nothing measures the filter side. Check three candidates: (1) BIP-110 signaling per block — the block `version` is in the WS block object, but nobody documented the bit mask; confirm the exact BIP9 check (top 3 bits `001` + bit 4, i.e. `(version & 0xE0000010) === 0x20000010`) against a known Ocean-signaled block. (2) Knots node share — "20%" (Lopp, Sybil-caveat) vs "25%" (Kratter) conflict, and neither cites a CORS-accessible API; check whether luke.dashjr.org/programs/bitcoin/files/charts/software.html or bitnodes.io API is fetchable client-side (CORS) or must be a hardcoded/manually-updated constant. (3) `extras.pool` (already in WS) for "Ocean mined this block" set-pieces — verified, usable.

**Classifier gaps**

- **BIP-110 test vectors never fetched.** Research names `bip-0110/test-vectors.json` in the bips repo as ground truth but no one pulled it or ran the 7 rules against it. Check: fetch it, confirm its schema (per-rule pass/fail cases?), and use it as the classifier's unit-test fixture. Without it, rules 3–6 ship unverified.
- **Zero sample txids that violate rules 3, 4, 5, or 6 in isolation** (annex: 0 observed; undefined witness versions, >257B control blocks, OP_SUCCESS: never searched for). If test vectors cover these, fine; otherwise the spec should mark those rules "implemented, untested against mainnet" and rely on synthetic fixtures.
- **Grandfathering is unimplementable as specced:** the API tx shape has `vin[].prevout` scriptpubkey/value but NOT the prevout's confirmation height, so per-input exemption would cost one extra REST call per input. The BIP isn't active anyway, so the spec should explicitly decide "apply rules 1–7 flat, no grandfathering" and say why — currently it's left as "either/or."
- **Rules 6–7 need a real tapscript opcode parser in JS; only Python exists** (rawscan.py/verify.py in the scratchpad). Not a research gap per se, but the spec must budget porting + the pitfall already documented (push payloads must not be scanned for opcodes).

**API verification gaps (small, cheap to close)**

- **`{"track-mempool":true}` initial-state behavior unknown.** Findings describe steady-state deltas (~5KB/s) but never say whether subscribing triggers an initial full-mempool dump (~90k txs would freeze a browser tab). Check: open the WS fresh, subscribe, and inspect the first `mempool-transactions` message size/shape.
- **WS `{"block":{...}}` push never observed live** — shape inferred from the init snapshot (no block mined in the 25-min test). Check: listen ~20 min more and confirm the message key and that it coincides with refreshed `mempool-blocks`.
- **Full-witness delivery for large txs over WS unverified.** The only verified `added[]` example is a small p2wpkh spend; nobody confirmed a multi-hundred-KB inscription reveal arrives with complete `vin[].witness` (vs truncated/omitted). Check: watch the stream until one ord/acme envelope tx appears and verify the witness matches `/api/tx/:txid`.
- **High-traffic behavior unknown:** track-mempool was measured at ~2 tx/s on a quiet mempool; docs warn "potentially a lot of data." No stress data for fee-spike periods (50+ tx/s → possibly 100KB/s+). Spec needs the already-proposed downgrade path (track-mempool-txids + sampled REST) stated as a mandatory auto-fallback with a concrete bandwidth threshold, not an option.
- **Aggregate-load / ToS question unanswered:** research validates ONE polite visitor; a public site multiplies that by concurrent visitors against mempool.space's unauthenticated tier. Check mempool.space's stated policy for third-party sites (docs/enterprise page) and enumerate fallback instances (e.g. mempool.emzy.de, mempool.bitcoin.de) with the same WS API for endpoint rotation — recon's "endpoint-rotation fallback" idea has no researched endpoint list.

**Data-honesty gaps**

- **Block sampling is biased:** `/txs, /txs/25, /txs/50, /txs/75` returns the first 100 txs in block order, which is roughly descending-feerate — inscriptions/runes cluster by feerate, so a "% of today's blockspace is spam" headline computed from that sample is skewed. Check: compare classifier prevalence on first-100 vs a full block (research already fully parsed 7 blocks — the comparison data exists in the scratchpad) and either quantify the bias or spec random page offsets.
- **Minor number conflicts to reconcile for on-screen text:** Knots share 20% vs 25% (pick one, cite it); hashrate signaling "near-zero" vs "0.31%/~5 EH/s" (use the number); author credit wording already resolved ("Dathon Ohm (pseudonym), original draft: Luke-Jr") — keep it.
- **`context_2026` string is truncated mid-sentence** in the compiled research ("from ~80/83 …") — recover the full 2026-context paragraph before writing lore/HUD copy, or the timeline section of the spec will be missing whatever followed.

**Explicitly adequate (no further research needed):** CORS (verified `*`), WS tx streaming (both track modes verified live), pagination form (path-segment, verified incl. failure modes), tx JSON shape incl. witness+prevouts, all six actively-circulating protocol detectors with verified txids and decode recipes (ord/BRC-20, Runes, Stamps/SRC-20, Counterparty ARC4 incl. key direction, ACME dual-encoding, large OP_RETURN), coinbase/THORChain false-positive exclusions, sim-fallback reference mechanics, and cast/copy material.